From fe18325c3eab3ca873f82eb1c8b2eee93b607cec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 11:42:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Read backslash escapes in a predicate format's quoted strings

A quoted literal in a predicate format was taken verbatim up to the next
quote character. macOS reads backslash escapes there: \\ is a backslash,
\" and \' are quotes that do not end the literal, \n, \t and \r are
control characters, \uXXXX, \xHH and \ooo name a character, and any other
escaped character is itself.

-predicateFormat writes a string constant with its backslashes and double
quotes escaped, so a predicate read back from its own description got
every backslash doubled, and one whose string held a double quote did not
parse at all. Core Data stores validation predicates as their formats, so
an attribute's pattern such as "gal \(US\)" came back as "gal \\(US\\)"
and refused the value it was written to accept.

The format walker that collects the variadic arguments skips quoted text;
it now skips an escaped quote too, so an argument after one is not lost.

Tests/base/NSPredicate/stringEscapes.m covers each escape, a pattern and a
quoted string surviving their description, and an argument after an
escaped quote; eleven of its thirteen checks fail without the change.
